fter_executor:node1:boot-identity","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes were directly demonstrated before the mandatory restart: snapshot-capable storage was established for /srv/data, a snapshot was taken, the directory was materially changed, and its exact earlier content and metadata were restored. The independent restart protocol was incomplete and does not change task functionality because restart persistence was not part of the public contract, but it materially limits confidence in the final operational state.","limitations":["The restart protocol lacks a different post-restart boot identity and recovered service, mount, data, and system-health observations. The fixed baseline boot identity was d052eb57-c81d-4309-ae7f-bce362073ff9, while the after-executor boot-identity probe received a connection reset.","Because node1 remained unreachable after reboot, persistence of the new loop-backed LVM activation, /srv/data mount, and restored data across restart is indeterminate.","After-executor lifecycle probes could not distinguish a node boot failure from a management-channel failure, and could not inspect failed services, listening sockets, temporary residue, or package health."]}
